// Generated by build-figures.cjs.
const fieldFigures = {"sur-116":[{"step":"A-frame. Run a low ridgeline between two sound anchors where permitted. Center the tarp over it so both panels shed water. Stake the four corners outward and alternate sides as you tension them. Leave enough room for the sleeping bag to clear the wet fabric.","title":"A-frame in cross section","alt":"From the end, the tarp forms two sloping panels. The ridgeline is overhead and the sleeping pad is between the low edges.","png":""},{"step":"Lean-to. Secure one reinforced long edge to a ridgeline or two supports. Stake the opposite long edge low on the side facing the wind. Viewed from the end, the tarp is a single slope with the open side facing away from the wind. If rain blows through the opening, lower the high edge or switch to an A-frame.","title":"Lean-to in cross section","alt":"Wind meets the staked low edge. The tarp rises to its supported edge, leaving the opening on the sheltered side.","png":""},{"step":"Plow-point. On a square tarp, secure one reinforced corner to a low anchor. Stake the opposite corner close to the ground toward the wind, then spread and stake the two side corners. Place your head toward the roomier end, still inside the drip line.","title":"Plow-point with one high corner","alt":"An oblique view of a square tarp. One raised corner forms the entrance. Two side corners and the far corner meet the ground.","png":""},{"step":"Test the pitch before moving bedding in. Look for a pocket where rain can pool. Move the nearest stake outward to smooth the panel, then check that runoff will pass beside the sleeping area. At home, pour a little water on the roof and watch where it lands.","title":"Check for a rain pocket","alt":"Two side profiles show a sag with water trapped in its middle, and a smooth taut panel that sheds water to the edge.","png":""}],"sur-128":[{"step":"Lay the baseplate edge from your known starting point toward the destination. The direction-of-travel arrow must point toward the destination. Rotate the dial until its orienting lines run parallel to the map\u2019s true north\u2013south direction, with the north end toward the top of that reference. Read the true bearing at the index.","title":"Point the baseplate from start to destination","alt":"A baseplate edge lies on the line from A at lower left to B at upper right. The travel arrow points toward B. The dial lines run north\u2013south on the map.","png":""},{"step":"On a compass without adjustable declination, convert the true bearing to a magnetic bearing: subtract east declination or add west declination. For practice, 80\u00b0 true with 5\u00b0 east gives 75\u00b0 magnetic. Set that result at the index. With an adjustable compass already configured for local declination per its manual, use the true bearing directly.","title":"Two different north references","alt":"An east declination places magnetic north to the right of true north. A target clockwise from both makes a smaller angle from magnetic north. The sketch exaggerates the separation.","png":""}],"trv-118":[{"step":"Closely spaced brown contour lines = steep cliff/hillside; widely spaced contour lines = gentle flat terrain.","title":"Read the same height gain on two slopes","alt":"Two sets of contour lines have the same elevation interval. A shorter horizontal distance between successive lines means a steeper slope.","png":""},{"step":"V-shaped contour lines pointing UPHILL (toward higher numbers) = a valley, draw, or creek bed; U- or V-shaped contours pointing DOWNHILL = a ridge spur.","title":"A stream crosses the V","alt":"Each contour turns upstream as it crosses the stream. Higher contour elevations are toward the top. The stream flows downhill in the opposite direction.","png":""},{"step":"Hourglass shape between two peaks = a mountain saddle/pass (often the easiest route across a ridge).","title":"Find the saddle between two summits","alt":"Outer contours enclose two hills together. Separate inner rings mark each summit. The lower neck between the rings is the saddle.","png":""}],"sur-152":[{"step":"If you can swim comfortably, swim along the shoreline until the seaward pull eases, then angle back toward the beach away from the current. If you tire or make no progress, float and keep signaling.","title":"Escape path viewed from above","alt":"The current flows away from the beach. If able to swim, go parallel to the beach to clear the channel, then angle back. Float and signal if you cannot make progress.","png":""}],"trv-114":[{"step":"Lay a waterproof groundsheet/tarp underneath the tent floor, folding edges 2 inches UNDER the tent perimeter\u2014if the groundsheet sticks out past the tent walls, it acts as a funnel channeling rainwater directly under your tent floor.","title":"Keep the footprint under the floor","alt":"End view of a tent with the groundsheet edges tucked inside the floor perimeter. Rain from the fly lands outside the groundsheet.","png":""}],"trv-135":[{"step":"Pack in 3 zones: Bottom = lightweight bulky sleeping bag/puffy jacket; Middle against spine = heaviest dense items (water, food bear canister, stove); Top/outer pockets = rain jacket, first-aid kit, headlamp, and snacks.","title":"Where weight rides in a pack","alt":"A side cutaway places dense gear close to the back in the middle, light bulky gear below, and frequently used items at the top.","png":""}],"sur-115":[{"step":"Bowline (non-slip fixed loop): Form an overhand loop ('rabbit hole'), pass the working end up through the hole, around behind the standing line ('around the tree'), and back down through the hole; pull tight.","title":"Form the small crossing loop","alt":"First move only. The standing part continues upward. Lay the returning strand across its front to form a small loop, leaving a long free end to make the larger fixed loop.","png":""},{"step":"Bowline (non-slip fixed loop): Form an overhand loop ('rabbit hole'), pass the working end up through the hole, around behind the standing line ('around the tree'), and back down through the hole; pull tight.","title":"Tie the bowline in three moves","alt":"Follow the rope from left to right. Bring the working end up through the small loop, take it behind the standing part, then return it down through the small loop. The large lower loop stays open.","png":""},{"step":"Clove Hitch (fast post/pole anchor): Wrap rope around a pole, cross over itself to make an X, wrap around a second time, and tuck the working end under the top X wrap.","title":"Cross, wrap and tuck the clove hitch","alt":"Three front views of a horizontal post. Cross the working end over the first turn, take another turn around the post, then tuck the working end under the crossing strand.","png":""},{"step":"Trucker's Hitch (3:1 mechanical pulley): Tie an alpine butterfly or slip loop midway up the line, pass the working end around an anchor, thread back through the loop, pull down hard to cinch cargo/ridgeline taut, and lock with two half-hitches.","title":"Trace the return through the midline loop","alt":"Routing sketch before tying off. Take the working end around the second anchor, back through the loop in the standing line, then pull toward the second anchor to tension.","png":""},{"step":"Square Lashing (joining two poles at 90\u00b0): Start with a clove hitch on the vertical pole, wrap over-and-under both poles 4 times, wrap 2\u20133 tight 'frapping' turns between the poles to cinch tight, and finish with a clove hitch.","title":"Where the frapping turns go","alt":"A front view shows the right-angle poles with outer wraps around them. The contrasting frapping turns cinch the bundle in the space between the poles.","png":""},{"step":"Taut-Line Hitch (adjustable guyline tensioner): Wrap working end twice around the standing line inside the loop, then once outside above the wraps; slide to tighten or loosen tarp stakes.","title":"Place the taut-line wraps on the correct sides","alt":"Schematic layout. Trace the rope from a stake around to the standing line. Place the first two turns on the stake side and the finishing half hitch on the outside toward the shelter.","png":""}],"hh-008":[{"step":"Sketch doors and windows and identify two exits from each room where possible. Clear obstructions and check that intended exits open.","title":"Trace two ways out","alt":"Example ground-floor bedroom. From the bed one route passes through the hall and exterior door, and another uses an accessible bedroom escape window. Both lead outside.","png":""}],"hh-014":[{"step":"Stand the jug on a stable surface with its spout above the catch basin. Place soap and a clean drying supply on the clean side.","title":"Set the spout over the basin","alt":"A drinking-water jug sits on a stable table. Its spout projects beyond the edge directly over a catch basin. Soap and clean towels stay up on the clean surface.","png":""}],"rep-106":[{"step":"Turn a quarter-turn ball valve 90 degrees perpendicular to the pipe (or turn a wheel gate valve clockwise until snug).","title":"Read a lever-style ball valve","alt":"Top view of two lever-operated ball valves. A lever parallel to the water pipe is open. A lever across the pipe is closed.","png":""}],"rep-107":[{"step":"Lift the tank lid and check the water level: if water is spilling into the center overflow tube, lower the float adjustment screw/clip on the fill valve.","title":"Look inside a gravity toilet tank","alt":"A side cutaway identifies the float on the fill valve, the open overflow tube, and the flapper over the outlet at the bottom.","png":""}],"rep-109":[{"step":"Unscrew the two large slip-joint nuts at both ends of the U-bend by hand (use channel-locks gently with a rag if stuck) and lower the trap into the bucket.","title":"Identify the removable trap bend","alt":"The U-shaped bend under a sink retains water. Slip nuts at the inlet and outlet release this section. A bucket beneath catches the water.","png":""}],"rep-117":[{"step":"Align the airflow arrow printed on the filter frame pointing INTO the return duct (toward the blower/attic).","title":"Which way the filter faces","alt":"A pleated filter sits between the return grille and the blower. Its frame arrow points in the direction the air travels, toward the blower.","png":""}],"rep-147":[{"step":"Use the concave wheel of a spline roller to press the new rubber spline into the frame channel along two adjacent sides, pull the mesh gently taut (not bowed inward), and roll the remaining two sides.","title":"Seat window mesh with the spline","alt":"A section across the aluminum frame shows mesh pressed down into its channel by a round rubber spline. Excess screen extends on the outside for trimming.","png":""}],"med-134":[{"step":"Use clean fine-tipped tweezers to grasp the attached tick close to the skin. Reach past its rounded body to avoid squeezing it.","title":"Grip at the skin surface","alt":"Side view. Fine tweezer tips reach the attachment point just above the skin below the tick\u2019s body. The pull is straight up and steady.","png":""}],"hh-031":[{"step":"Place a room-sized air cleaner that does not produce ozone where its vents are clear. Run it continuously at the highest tolerable speed. Avoid frying, burning candles and smoking indoors.","title":"Give the air cleaner room to circulate","alt":"Plan view of a room with a closed exterior window, an unobstructed purifier, and a clear route to the door. Arrows show air circulating inside.","png":""}],"shp-132":[{"step":"Ignore front-of-package 'Family Size' claims and read the small per-ounce or per-count unit price tag on the shelf rail.","title":"Compare the price for the same unit","alt":"Two example shelf tags show a twelve-ounce pack at three dollars and a twenty-ounce pack at four dollars. Dividing price by ounces gives twenty-five and twenty cents per ounce.","png":""}],"shp-151":[{"step":"For each quote enter materials, labor, temporary protection, disposal, permits where applicable, schedule, payment stages and warranty. Mark omitted items as unknown instead of zero.","title":"Spot a missing item in a repair quote","alt":"Example comparison sheet. Quote A includes hauling. Quote B leaves hauling unspecified. A question mark flags the cost to confirm before comparing totals.","png":""}],"hom-109":[{"step":"Lay 1/4-inch drip lines in parallel rows down each bed, pinning with landscape staples and folding/clamping the ends.","title":"Read a drip bed from above","alt":"A header runs across one end of a bed. Smaller drip laterals branch off it and run in parallel to capped ends.","png":""}],"hom-110":[{"step":"Bury the pot up to its neck in the center of a tomato, pepper, or squash planting area.","title":"Bury an olla with the opening accessible","alt":"Cross section shows a covered clay pot buried to its neck. Water stays inside the pot and passes through its porous sidewall into the root zone.","png":""}],"hom-115":[{"step":"Snip a 4\u20136 inch healthy green stem tip early in the morning using sterilized bypass pruners, cutting just below a leaf node.","title":"Find the node before taking a cutting","alt":"A stem has leaves emerging at nodes. A dashed cut line sits immediately below a node. On the prepared cutting, lower leaves have been stripped while leaves remain at the tip.","png":""}],"hom-127":[{"step":"Shape stone fruits (peaches/plums) into an open vase with 3\u20134 outward scaffold limbs so sunlight and coastal breezes dry leaves quickly.","title":"Open-center shape for stone fruit","alt":"Side view of a vase-shaped tree. Scaffold limbs angle outward from the trunk and leave daylight in the middle.","png":""}],"hom-135":[{"step":"Slip 10-foot PVC pipes over opposite rebar stakes to create a 4-foot-tall walk-under tunnel.","title":"Hoops span the bed","alt":"Perspective sketch of a row of curved hoops. The two feet of each hoop sit opposite one another on separate sides of the raised bed.","png":""}],"hom-136":[{"step":"Nest the buckets, pack moist potting mix tightly into the center wicking net cup, fill the bucket with potting mix, and top with a plastic mulch cover.","title":"Cutaway of a self-watering planter","alt":"Water enters through the fill tube. A soil-filled wick reaches below the inner floor to the water. The side overflow sets the highest water level and leaves an air space.","png":""}],"hom-151":[{"step":"Sketch the locations and depths. Look for a pattern such as a dry edge or overlapping spray in the middle.","title":"Map the catch-cup readings","alt":"An example bed plan shows four cups with water depths after the same run. The far and dry-edge cups contain less water than the middle cup.","png":""}],"fwg-103":[{"step":"Drape 40%\u201350% Aluminet reflective shade cloth OVER THE EXTERIOR roof from May through September\u2014blocking sunlight outside the roof drops interior temps by 12\u00b0F\u201318\u00b0F (hanging shade cloth inside still traps solar heat).","title":"Put shade cloth outside the glazing","alt":"Cross section shows shade cloth spanning the outside face of a greenhouse roof. Sun meets the cloth before it reaches the glazing.","png":""}],"fwg-112":[{"step":"Squash, Melons & Cucumbers (separate male and female flowers): Pluck a male flower (thin straight stem), peel back petals, and dab its pollen-covered anther directly onto the center of the female flower (which has a tiny baby fruit behind the petals).","title":"Look behind a squash blossom","alt":"Both blossoms have petals. A male blossom has a thin stalk under the petals. A female blossom has a small immature squash between the petals and the stalk.","png":""}],"fwg-118":[{"step":"As plants drink water over 4 weeks, the water level drops and leaves an air gap for upper oxygen roots\u2014growing crisp salad heads in 28 days with zero pumps or timers.","title":"The air gap in a Kratky container","alt":"A side cutaway shows a plant held in a net cup. As water drops, upper roots occupy an air space while lower roots still reach the nutrient solution.","png":""}],"fwg-121":[{"step":"Record both overnight minima and outside conditions. Swap the instruments on a similar night to see whether the difference follows the site or the thermometer.","title":"Swap sensors to check a cold spot","alt":"In the first trial thermometer A is at the bench and B at the door. On the next comparable night their locations reverse. A cold reading that remains at the door implicates that spot.","png":""}],"kit-125":[{"step":"Stir in cold buttermilk until shaggy, turn onto a floured board, pat into a rectangle, and fold in thirds like a letter 3 times (laminating flaky layers).","title":"Fold biscuit dough like a letter","alt":"Divide the rectangle into thirds. Fold one outside third over the center, then bring the remaining third over the stack. The end view shows three layers.","png":""}],"kit-201":[{"step":"Brush with softened butter, slice into 12 pizza wedges, roll wide-to-tip into crescents, proof 40 minutes, and bake at 375\u00b0F for 14-16 minutes.","title":"Roll a crescent from the wide edge","alt":"A triangular piece of dough points away from you. Roll the wide base toward the small tip, leaving the tip tucked beneath the shaped roll.","png":""}],"kit-165":[{"step":"Roll into a 12x18-inch rectangle, spread softened butter and cinnamon-sugar, roll tightly, and slice into 12 rolls using unflavored dental floss or a sharp serrated knife.","title":"Slice across the rolled log","alt":"A cinnamon log is cut perpendicular to its long axis. The separated piece shows the spiral that faces upward in the pan.","png":""}],"kit-063":[{"step":"Whisk oil and flour in a heavy Dutch oven over medium-low heat constantly for 25-35 minutes until the roux turns the color of dark melted milk chocolate.","title":"Watch the roux deepen","alt":"Three swatches represent flour and oil at blond, copper and dark brown stages. The target here is an even dark brown across the pan. Lighting can shift the apparent shade.","png":""}],"crf-121":[{"step":"Run auto bed mesh leveling at printing bed temperature (60\u00b0C for PLA, 80\u00b0C for PETG) and fine-tune live Z-offset until extruded lines squish flat together with zero gaps between lines and zero rough ridges.","title":"Read a first-layer test patch","alt":"Top views of a first layer show rounded separate tracks when the nozzle is high, smooth touching tracks at the desired height, and rough ridges when the nozzle is too low.","png":""}],"crf-128":[{"step":"1. Backstitch (strongest seam repair): Bring needle up, stitch backward one stitch length, then advance two stitch lengths underneath\u2014creates a continuous machine-strength line.","title":"Backstitch seen from the side","alt":"With the fabric shown edge-on, come up at B, go back down at A, travel underneath past B, and emerge at C. The next top stitch returns from C to B.","png":""},{"step":"2. Ladder / Slip Stitch (invisible pillow/plush/hem closure): Fold raw edges inside, alternate horizontal bites inside each folded crease, and pull gently\u2014stitches disappear completely.","title":"Alternate bites along two folds","alt":"Two folded fabric edges face each other. The thread runs inside one fold, crosses the gap, runs inside the other fold and crosses back. These exposed crossbars draw the opening shut.","png":""}],"crf-132":[{"step":"Swiss / Woven Darning for Socks & Sweaters: Stretch the hole gently over a darning mushroom, weave vertical warp threads across the hole anchoring 1/2-inch into sound knit fabric, then weave horizontal weft threads over-and-under like a tiny loom.","title":"Darn across both directions","alt":"Threads bridge a hole vertically and anchor in sound fabric. A second set passes over and under on alternating rows to rebuild the missing area.","png":""}],"crf-146":[{"step":"Pin-baste the 3-layer quilt sandwich every 4\u20135 inches from the center outward, and quilt from the center out toward edges.","title":"Stack a quilt before basting","alt":"An exploded edge view shows the quilt top face up, the batting in the middle and the backing face down, with its finished side facing the table.","png":""}],"crf-165":[{"step":"Overlap elastic ends by 1 inch (never twist!) and sew a sturdy box-X stitch across the overlap, close the casing gap, and stitch vertically in the ditch at side seams so elastic never rolls inside the waistband.","title":"Lock the elastic overlap with a box-X","alt":"Two flat ends overlap. A rectangle is stitched within the overlap, with two diagonal stitch lines forming an X inside the rectangle.","png":""}],"crf-167":[{"step":"Pinch each bottom corner into a triangle aligning side seam to bottom seam, sew 1.5 inches across the tip to box the corners, turn right-side out through the lining gap, and stitch the lining shut.","title":"Box a pouch corner","alt":"With seams aligned, the flattened corner is a triangle. The new stitch line runs across that triangle, perpendicular to the center seam.","png":""}],"crf-176":[{"step":"Clean and prepare the area as the patch maker directs. Cut rounded corners and allow the specified overlap beyond both ends of the tear.","title":"Extend the patch past both tear tips","alt":"A rounded rectangular patch surrounds a zigzag tear. Both tips are well inside the patch. The required overlap depends on the patch maker.","png":""}],"kit-003":[{"step":"Place the wrapped bundle between two flat plates weighted with a heavy cast-iron skillet for 30-45 minutes until firm enough to cube.","title":"Press the wrapped paneer evenly","alt":"In side view, a centered weight rests on a flat top plate, with the cloth-wrapped curds between the plates. Escaping whey gathers in the lower dish.","png":""}],"kit-006":[{"step":"Chill incubated yogurt for 4 hours, then strain through butter muslin for 2 hours for Greek yogurt\u2014or 12-24 hours with 1/2 tsp salt stirred in for spreadable labneh.","title":"Keep the strainer clear of the whey","alt":"Cross section shows yogurt in cloth supported by a sieve over a deeper bowl. Liquid drains into the space below, away from the yogurt. Keep the setup refrigerated.","png":""}],"kit-084":[{"step":"Pulse flour, salt, sugar, and ice-cold cubed butter until butter pieces are the size of peas and flat flakes (visible butter flakes create steam pockets).","title":"What to leave in the flour","alt":"A close-up shows pea-size butter pieces alongside thin flat flakes in the flour. Visible pieces are the texture checkpoint before adding water.","png":""}],"kit-221":[{"step":"Roll chilled pie dough over the top, crimp edges, cut 4 steam vents, brush with egg wash, and bake at 400\u00b0F for 30-35 minutes until golden brown.","title":"Cut vents through the top crust","alt":"Overhead view of a pie showing four short slits near its center. Each slit passes through the top crust so steam can escape.","png":""}],"kit-168":[{"step":"Shape gently into a round loaf on parchment, slash a deep 1/2-inch cross across the top (helps heat reach the thick center), and bake at 425\u00b0F for 35-40 minutes until hollow-sounding when tapped.","title":"Score the round loaf","alt":"Overhead view of a round dough with two long crossing cuts through the center. The scoring leaves four sections joined at the base.","png":""}],"kit-229":[{"step":"Freeze soups and sauces flat on a sheet pan in zip or chamber bags so they stack like books in the freezer.","title":"Freeze flat, then file upright","alt":"A sealed portion lies flat on a tray to freeze. Once solid, flat portions stand upright together with their labels facing out.","png":""}],"hom-121":[{"step":"In October\u2013November, separate bulbs into individual cloves (leaving papery wrappers intact) and plant pointed-end up, 2 inches deep and 6 inches apart.","title":"Orient a garlic clove","alt":"Soil cross section shows a clove with the pointed tip facing upward and the broad basal plate downward, where roots emerge.","png":""}],"crf-102":[{"step":"Measure your laser's exact kerf (the width of material vaporized by the beam, typically 0.006\"\u20130.008\" on a Glowforge) by cutting a 1.000-inch test square and measuring the resulting piece with digital calipers (1.000\" minus measured width = kerf).","title":"Kerf is the missing strip","alt":"Magnified view across a cut. The drawn toolpath runs down the middle of a narrow removed strip. The solid material starts at the two edges of the gap.","png":""}],"crf-105":[{"step":"Generate or import an overlapping staggered-slot living-hinge vector pattern where cut lines run PARALLEL to the outer wood grain direction (bending perpendicular to the outer veneer fibers prevents snapping).","title":"Stagger slots to leave connecting bridges","alt":"Top view of a living-hinge field shows parallel slots. Gaps in adjacent rows are offset so narrow uncut bridges connect the sheet.","png":""}],"crf-117":[{"step":"Multi-Color Layering Alignment: Add two small alignment squares/stars in the exact same position on every color layer in Design Space; layer colors onto clear transfer tape using a sheet of parchment paper slipped underneath (leaving only the registration stars exposed) until locked in place.","title":"Register two vinyl layers","alt":"Two example layers carry matching square marks in the same positions. When the squares land exactly over each other, the internal motifs share the same alignment.","png":""}],"trv-123":[{"step":"Adjust pole length on flat ground so your elbow forms a comfortable 90\u00b0 angle when holding the grip; shorten poles by 5\u201310 cm on steep uphill climbs and lengthen by 5\u201310 cm on steep downhills to take up to 25% of impact off your knees.","title":"Set pole height on level ground","alt":"Side view of an upper arm hanging down and a forearm level with the ground, meeting at a right angle. The hand holds the pole vertically.","png":""}],"trv-134":[{"step":"Hang straps at a 30\u00b0 angle (finger-gun thumb-to-index angle) and set a structural ridgeline at 83% of hammock length so the hammock has a comfortable sag\u2014lie diagonally at 15\u00b0\u201320\u00b0 across the hammock to sleep completely flat like a bed.","title":"Measure suspension from horizontal","alt":"Side view shows the strap sloping down from its tree anchor to the hammock. Its angle is measured against an imaginary horizontal line drawn from that anchor.","png":""}],"hh-032":[{"step":"Leave the electricity, cooling, medicine storage and medical equipment running throughout this tabletop exercise. On one page draw seven day columns and make rows for drinking water, meals, cooling, medicine or powered equipment, and contact with someone outside the house.","title":"Sketch a week on one sheet","alt":"Example worksheet has a column for each day and rows for water, meals, cooling, medicine or equipment, and check-ins. Put a question mark in a cell as soon as a supply or arrangement becomes uncertain.","png":""}],"med-176":[{"step":"Add one plain-language direction from the street to the workshop or garden. Keep sensitive access codes out of public view.","title":"Sketch the approach to the entrance","alt":"Fictional example. A sketch marks the street, side gate and workshop entrance. A helper can point out the same path from the street.","png":""}],"sur-101":[{"step":"In a safe spot, stop moving. Mark the time, check your last known landmark and inventory water, shelter, light and communication.","title":"The STOP sequence in a safe spot","alt":"Four linked steps: Sit down, Think, Observe surroundings and resources, then Plan before moving.","png":""}],"sur-104":[{"step":"For splitting wrist-thick dry kindling (batoning): rest the blade edge on the wood grain, strike ONLY the protruding tip/spine with a wooden baton (never a rock or metal hammer), and never twist the blade sideways.","title":"Safe batoning strike point","alt":"A wooden baton strikes the protruding tip of a full-tang knife resting level across a log on a solid base.","png":""}],"sur-105":[{"step":"In a dry creek bed, dig 1\u20132 feet deep at the OUTSIDE bend of a curve (where fast water scoured deepest) until water seeps in; let sediment settle before scooping.","title":"Where to dig in a dry riverbed","alt":"Top view of a winding dry creek bed showing the dig hole on the deeper outside bend rather than the shallow inside sandbar.","png":""}],"sur-106":[{"step":"Solar ground still: Dig a 3-ft-wide by 2-ft-deep bowl in sunny moist soil, place a catch cup in the center, pack chopped green vegetation around the cup, cover the pit with clear plastic sealed with soil around the rim, and place a small stone in the center directly over the cup.","title":"Tree transpiration bag and solar ground still","alt":"Side-by-side diagrams of a clear plastic transpiration bag on a leafy branch and a solar ground still dripping into a catch cup.","png":""}],"sur-109":[{"step":"Gather 3x more fuel than you think you need BEFORE striking a spark, sorting into four distinct piles off the damp ground.","title":"Three-stage fire fuel preparation","alt":"Three sorted fuel piles increasing in thickness from fine tinder to pencil-thick kindling and wrist-thick fuelwood.","png":""}],"sur-110":[{"step":"Place the tip of your ferro rod directly into the tinder/curls, hold the 90\u00b0 knife spine steady, and pull the ferro rod BACKWARD away from the pile\u2014scraping showers of 5,000\u00b0F sparks straight into the tinder without knocking your nest apart.","title":"Pulling the ferro rod backward into feather curls","alt":"The knife spine stays steady at the tinder curls while the ferrocerium rod is pulled backward to shower sparks without scattering tinder.","png":""}],"sur-112":[{"step":"Bow drill: Carve a thumb-thick spindle and flat hearth board from the same dry non-resinous wood, burn a shallow depression, then cut a 1/8-pie V-notch right to the center of the depression.","title":"Bow-drill spindle, hearth notch, and bearing block","alt":"Cross-section of a bow-drill setup showing the bearing block on top, bow cord wrapped around the spindle, and V-notch in the hearth board.","png":""}],"sur-117":[{"step":"Pile at least 2 to 3 FEET (arm's-length thick!) of dry leaves, grass, and bark over the entire frame, and stuff a backpack or leaf-filled sack in the entrance doorway at night.","title":"Cross-section of a natural debris hut","alt":"End-view cross-section showing a snug interior space over a thick dry-leaf mattress insulated by 2 to 3 feet of piled leaves.","png":""}],"sur-119":[{"step":"Dress in 3 modular layers: (1) moisture-wicking merino wool or polyester against skin; (2) insulating fleece, wool, or down; (3) breathable wind/rain shell.","title":"Three-layer cold-weather clothing system","alt":"Diagram of the three modular clothing layers: moisture-wicking base, insulating mid-layer, and wind/waterproof shell.","png":""}],"sur-120":[{"step":"Create a double-roof air-gap shade canopy using two tarps spaced 12 inches apart so upper heat radiates away instead of baking underneath.","title":"Double-roof air-gap desert shade canopy","alt":"Two tarps rigged 12 inches apart so breeze carries heat out of the gap while keeping the shaded area off scorching sand.","png":""}],"sur-129":[{"step":"Slide one lower bead on your Ranger pace-count string every 100 meters; after 10 lower beads (1 km), slide one upper kilometer bead.","title":"Ranger pace-count beads layout","alt":"Diagram of Ranger pace beads showing nine lower 100-meter beads separated by a center knot from four upper 1-kilometer beads.","png":""}],"sur-130":[{"step":"Shadow-Stick Method: Push a 3-ft stick straight into flat sunny ground, place Pebble #1 at the exact tip of the shadow, wait 15\u201320 minutes, and place Pebble #2 at the new shadow tip\u2014a line drawn from Pebble #1 to Pebble #2 points straight WEST-TO-EAST anywhere on Earth.","title":"Shadow-stick East-West line and Big Dipper pointer to Polaris","alt":"Left: two shadow-tip pebbles form a West-to-East line. Right: extending the Big Dipper outer cup stars five times points to Polaris.","png":""}],"sur-132":[{"step":"Ground-to-Air Search & Rescue Codes (make letters at least 10 ft tall with high color contrast in an open clearing): V = Require Assistance; X = Require Medical Assistance; N = No / Negative; Y = Yes / Affirmative; \u2192 = Proceeding in this direction.","title":"Ground-to-air search and rescue signal codes","alt":"Four standard ground-to-air signals at least 10 feet tall: V for assistance, X for medical aid, N for no, and Y for yes.","png":""}],"sur-135":[{"step":"Follow SWOC in seconds: (1) SEATBELTS off immediately (adults unbuckle themselves first, then children); (2) WINDOWS rolled down or shattered at the corner with a spring-loaded punch (tempered side windows shatter easily; laminated windshields will NOT break); (3) OUT immediately\u2014children pushed out first, followed by adults; (4) CLIMB onto the car roof or swim with the current toward high ground.","title":"Submerged vehicle SWOC escape sequence","alt":"Four-step vehicle water escape: Seatbelts off, Windows opened or punched at side corner, Out immediately, Climb to safety.","png":""}],"sur-141":[{"step":"Keep urine (liquid) and feces (solid) in SEPARATE buckets\u2014separating liquids eliminates 95% of odor and prevents overflowing bags.","title":"Twin-bucket off-grid sanitation separation","alt":"Two labeled 5-gallon buckets separating liquid waste from bagged solid waste layered with sawdust or litter.","png":""}],"sur-145":[{"step":"If stranded in cold water wearing a PFD, assume the HELP (Heat Escape Lessening Posture): cross arms tight over chest, clamp thighs together, and pull knees up toward chest to protect high-heat-loss groin, armpits, and chest.","title":"HELP cold-water heat-escape lessening posture","alt":"Side view of a person wearing a life jacket floating with arms crossed over the chest and knees pulled up to conserve core heat.","png":""}],"trv-115":[{"step":"PCT Method (impossible for bears to untie from the trunk): Reach as high as possible on the rope, tie a clove hitch around a 4-inch toggle stick, and slowly lower the food bag until the toggle jams against the carabiner\u2014suspending the food 12 feet high and 6 feet out with no rope tied to the tree trunk.","title":"PCT bear-bag hang with toggle stick","alt":"A dry food bag suspended 12 feet off the ground and 6 feet from the tree trunk using a clove-hitched toggle jammed against a carabiner.","png":""}],"trv-116":[{"step":"Dig a cathole 6 to 8 inches deep into dark organic topsoil (where microbes decompose waste rapidly\u2014not deep sterile clay), deposit waste, stir with a stick and dirt, and pack out used toilet paper in a double zip-top bag.","title":"Leave No Trace cathole depth and setback","alt":"Soil cross-section showing a 6 to 8 inch cathole dug into dark organic topsoil at least 200 feet from water and trails.","png":""}],"trv-119":[{"step":"Each finger width equals roughly 15 minutes of remaining daylight (4 fingers = 1 full hand width = 1 hour before sunset).","title":"Estimating sunset time with finger widths","alt":"Four horizontal fingers stacked between the bottom of the sun and the horizon equal roughly one hour of daylight.","png":""}],"trv-122":[{"step":"Dutch Oven 350\u00b0F Baking Rule ('Rule of 3s'): Take your Dutch oven diameter in inches (e.g., 12-inch), use twice that number of briquettes (24 total), and place 2/3 on the LID (16 coals) and 1/3 UNDERNEATH (8 coals) for even 350\u00b0F oven baking without burning bottoms.","title":"Dutch oven charcoal distribution for 350\u00b0F baking","alt":"Side view of a legged camp Dutch oven with two-thirds of the charcoal briquettes on the flanged lid and one-third underneath.","png":""}]};
if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) module.exports = fieldFigures;
